$, $b$, the optimiser and the multiplier be classified?","options":["$a$ and $b$ are exogenous; the optimiser and multiplier are endogenous.","$x$ is exogenous because the decision maker chooses its value.","$\\lambda$ is exogenous because the modeller introduces the multiplier.","All quantities are endogenous because the problem is an optimisation."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8921aaec527511b5b34ec5da","time_limit":45,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2b_math_iv_multivariate_calculus_exogenous_and_endogenous_variables_ae0cf746_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $F\\colon\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$ be $C^1$, with $F(y_0)=0$ and $\\operatorname{rank}DF(y_0)=2$. Initially all three coordinates of $y$ are designated endogenous. Which change permits the standard square implicit function theorem to describe the local solutions?","options":["Keep all three endogenous; rank two makes the $2\\times3$ Jacobian invertible.","Make exogenous a coordinate whose removal leaves two independent columns","Take any one coordinate as exogenous; every remaining $2\\times2$ block is invertible.","Take both equations as exogenous inputs; the three coordinates then become uniquely determined."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6c103ebbe6979f268e6de732","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}